{"step_index":690,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-25T01:37:39Z","content":"Created At: 2026-11-24T20:37:39-05:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-24T20:37:39-05:00\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/<user>/Downloads/GTA6.Xbox.v1.0.2.1-CYBERLEEK/output/SPEEDRUN_ROUTING_NOTES.md`\nTotal Lines: 180\nTotal Bytes: 12275\nShowing lines 1 to 150\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: # Speedrun routing notes: build 1.0.2.1 (Xbox Series)\r\n2: \r\n3: ## Status\r\n4: \r\n5: **The mission progression graph has not been reconstructed.** No mission prerequisite, trigger, or protagonist\r\n6: requirement was recovered. What was recovered is a verified mission inventory, a chapter grouping from two\r\n7: independent sources, the progress-menu display order, four replay-entry links, and a small set of flow facts\r\n8: read from a separately supplied folder of decompiled scripts.\r\n9: \r\n10: Do not route from these files as if they described unlock order. In `mission_order.json`, `null` means unknown.\r\n11: \r\n12: **Readable mission titles are not available.** Neither the dump nor the decompiled scripts contain display text.\r\n13: The names in these files are the game's internal mission IDs.\r\n14: \r\n15: ## What was recovered\r\n16: \r\n17: | Finding | Source | Confidence |\r\n18: |---|---|---|\r\n19: | 145 mission table entries (ID, replay text key, grid texture) | `common.rpf` @ `0x219000`, XML root `CMissions` | Observed |\r\n20: | 97 replay-menu entries under five chapter filters | `data.rpf` @ `0x12010000`, RBF0 root `uiProgressDataset`, category `missions_card` | Observed |\r\n21: | Chapter grouping: 22 / 13 / 28 / 27 / 7 entries for chapters 1 to 5 | Menu filters `missions_chapter_1` to `_5` | Observed |\r\n22: | Second chapter source: `CH1` to `CH5` tag in each grid texture name | `CMissions` `TextureName` | Observed, agrees for 87 of 92 comparable entries |\r\n23: | Four replay entries keyed to another mission | Menu item `ID` differs from `UnlockId` | Observed, meaning inferred |\r\n24: | Build version 1.0.2.1 | `appxmanifest.xml`, `MicrosoftGame.Config` | Observed |\r\n25: \r\n26: The 97 menu entries and the 145 table rows overlap on 95 IDs. Two menu entries have no table row\r\n27: (`CTI_PIL_RD_SH`, `MTL_AMBUL`) and 50 table rows have no menu entry.\r\n28: \r\n29: ## Decompiled script evidence\r\n30: \r\n31: Source: `C:\\Users\\<user>\\Downloads\\americas_scripts_23_11_2026` (746 `.ysc.cs` files, 52.3 million lines, read\r\n32: only). This folder is not part of the dump. Its link to this build is that the joaat hashes of 144 of the 145\r\n33: `CMissions` IDs occur in it as constants, against 0 hits for 145 control strings.\r\n34: \r\n35: | Finding | Where | Confidence |\r\n36: |---|---|---|\r\n37: | No display text. Strings appear only as `Vm.StringConstant(program, heapOffset)` (55 lines) and the heap is not in the folder; every quoted literal is decompiler vocabulary | All files, `Runtime\\Vm.cs` | Observed |\r\n38: | 196 scripts reference a mission ID; 177 of them reference exactly one | `work\\decomp_mission_refs.json` | Observed |\r\n39: | 95 of 97 replay-menu missions have at least one such script (not checked: `CTI_PIL_RD_SH`, `MTL_AMBUL`, which have no `CMissions` row) | `decompiled_mission_scripts_referencing_id` in the JSON | Observed |\r\n40: | `func_109E09` returns 1 for 131 mission IDs. The 14 left out are mostly IDs that have variant rows (`AIR_CATER`, `AIR_FINAL`, `BIL_MOTEL`, `BIL_PENT`, `BIL_PICK`, `INT_PRISO`, `ROB_BOAT_J`, `FT_UP_MMRENT`, `ANT_INTRO`, `FT_AMUCO`, `FT_KMODSHP`, `COM_KBOS_PLNDATE`) plus `ANT_GIVE` and `CTI_PIL_RD_BC_PARENT` | `0xD2601E252151E04B.ysc.cs` lines 570880 to 572796 | Observed |\r\n41: | `func_10AD05` returns true if the flag for `BIL_FINALE_M` **or** `BIL_FINAL_B` is set | Same file, lines 573115 and 573127 | Observed |\r\n42: | `func_10AA55(id)` called with `MTL_WAKE` sets the flags of `FT_KMODSHP_SECOND`, `COM_KBOS_SHOP`, `COM_KBOS_COLAT`, `COM_KBOS_RADR`, `COM_KBOS_NEIGHB`, `COM_KBOS_CLEAN`, `HO_PAR_KEYCO`, `RecoverDropTipShackDonnieAlive`, `RecoverDropTipShackDonnieDeadUnknown` | Same file, lines 572964 to 573008 | Observed |\r\n43: | `func_10AA55(id)` called with `MNY_HOTEL` or `MNY_HOTEL_AUTOLAUNCH` sets `ANT_SRCH` and `ANT_RECOV`; sets `ANT_CHASE` unless `ANT_CHASE_AUTOLAUNCH` or `ANT_CHASE` is already set; sets `ANT_INTRO_APARTMENT` unless `ANT_INTRO_APARTMENT` or `ANT_INTRO_STORAGE` is already set | Same file, lines 572946 to 573082 | Observed |\r\n44: | `BIL_PICK_NIGHT_TIME_START` and `BIL_PICK_DAY_TIME_START` are passed to the same native with a boolean and its negation; the boolean includes a `< 17` comparison | `story000.ysc.cs` lines 116320 to 116328 | Observed |\r\n45: \r\n46: \"Flag\" means the per-ID value written by `Virtual.type_37658EE3464E5234.Method_0(store, id, 1)` and read by\r\n47: `Method_1`. The methods are unnamed. Reading the flag as \"mission completed\" is an **inference** from the ID names\r\n48: and from `func_10AD05`; it is not proven. Whether `func_10AA55` runs at mission start or mission pass is unknown:\r\n49: no direct call to it was found in the decompiled code, so it is presumably dispatched indirectly.\r\n50: \r\n51: If that inference holds, it would mean the listed chapter 1 entries are closed out when `MTL_WAKE` (first entry of\r\n52: the chapter 2 filter) is processed, the `ANT_*` entries are closed out when `MNY_HOTEL` (first entry of the\r\n53: chapter 4 filter) is processed, and the story has two alternative final missions. Treat all three as leads to test,\r\n54: not as routing facts.\r\n55: \r\n56: Eight scripts reference IDs of more than one mission family. What each reference does was not determined:\r\n57: `0x2CC10383B818622E` (`HO_PAR_FRINT`, `MTL_MUD`), `0x4C79572EA44F9250` (`AO_SHOW_L_EXP`,\r\n58: `robbergetawaydriverprep`), `0x69B5F3FBB0807F99` (`COM_KBOS_COLAT`, `CTI_MECH_STFC`, `CTI_SHOW_HATH`),\r\n59: `0x947D998BD81F7AC1` (`AIR_TAIL`, `CLB_EVI`, `FT_KMODSHP_FIRST`, `FT_KMODSHP_SECOND`), `0x96FAE5BE63A12676`\r\n60: (`BIL_BOSS`, `COA_HELIS`), `0xCB466D0841607AEC` (`COM_MECH_INTR`, `COM_SFH_MVFN`), `0xCBB412F4549E13F9`\r\n61: (`FT_MOTEL_SHOP`, `FT_UP_MMRENT_MANAGER_*`, `UP_MLSPM`), `0xD6DA4FF46709E305` (`INT_PLNSH`, `MTL_CONT`).\r\n62: \r\n63: Not analysed: `func_12EB6F` (maps unnamed hashes to 17 mission IDs), `func_24BCE4` (registers a record for each\r\n64: of 31 mission IDs), and the natives that take a mission ID (`_0x65F45909812A5248`, `_0x7191C011FD1040FD`,\r\n65: `_0xB0584804833510A0`, `_0x480014E54CD33903`, `_0xC663AE0D5D968A80`), whose names are not in the decompilation.\r\n66: \r\n67: ## Structural topology summary\r\n68: \r\n69: Unknown. The readable data contains no linear-versus-parallel information. The only structural hint is the pair\r\n70: of alternative final-mission flags above.\r\n71: \r\n72: - Menu order inside a chapter is a display order. `UniqueMissionIndex` is not chronological either: index 23 is\r\n73:   tagged `CH3`, index 67 is `CH5`, and indices 98 and 99 are `CH3`.\r\n74: - Mission entries use `uiUnlockEvaluatorMission` with no parameters, so the menu holds no condition expressions.\r\n75: \r\n76: ## Hard synchronization bottlenecks\r\n77: \r\n78: None determinable. No mission could be shown to gate another.\r\n79: \r\n80: ## Speedrun-critical mechanics found in scripts\r\n81: \r\n82: None confirmed. The dump's own script archives were not readable. The decompiled folder gave the flag facts in\r\n83: the section above and nothing about phone-call delays, timers, fail-skip thresholds, or stat and cash gates.\r\n84: \r\n85: The following are **names only**, taken from table rows that share a listed mission's ID as a prefix. They show\r\n86: that variants exist. They do not show what triggers them or what they change.\r\n87: \r\n88: | Listed mission | Variant table entries |\r\n89: |---|---|\r\n90: | `INT_PRISO` | `_TRIGGER_EARLY`, `_TRIGGER_LATE` |\r\n91: | `BIL_PICK` | `_DAY_TIME_START`, `_NIGHT_TIME_START` |\r\n92: | `MNY_HOTEL` | `_AUTOLAUNCH` |\r\n93: | `MNY_KIDNP` | `_AUTO-LAUNCH` |\r\n94: | `BIL_MOTEL` | `_NON_VIOLENT`, `_VIOLENT` |\r\n95: | `AIR_FINAL` | `_DATA_HACK_FAILED`, `_DATA_HACK_PASSED` |\r\n96: | `AIR_CATER` | `_DHF`, `_DHP`, `_DHS` |\r\n97: | `BIL_PENT` | `_B_BRIAN`, `_M_MENDEZ` |\r\n98: | `ROB_BOAT_J` | `_JASON` (a separate row `ROB_BOAT_L_LUCIA` also exists) |\r\n99: | `FT_AMUCO` | `_FIRST_INTERACTION`, `_SECOND_INTERACTION` |\r\n100: | `FT_KMODSHP` | `_FIRST`, `_SECOND` |\r\n101: | `ANT_INTRO` | `_APARTMENT`, `_STORAGE` |\r\n102: | `COM_KBOS_PLNDATE` | `_PLANE_N_DINNER_DATE` |\r\n103: \r\n104: A further 27 table rows match no listed mission by prefix, including `ANT_CHASE_AUTOLAUNCH`, `BIL_FINALE_M`,\r\n105: `BIL_FINAL_B`, `FIN_CONT`, `INT_CRED`, and four `FT_UP_MMRENT_MANAGER_*` rows. The full list is in\r\n106: `cmissions_entries_not_in_replay_menu`.\r\n107: \r\n108: ## Replay-entry links\r\n109: \r\n110: These are the only cross-mission relations in the readable data. In each case the menu entry's `UnlockId` names a\r\n111: different mission than its own `ID`.\r\n112: \r\n113: | Replay entry | `UnlockId` |\r\n114: |---|---|\r\n115: | `BIL_PICK` | `BIL_WHEEL` |\r\n116: | `BIL_HOUSE` | `BIL_WHEEL` |\r\n117: | `BAN_SETUP` | `BAN_RESCU` |\r\n118: | `MNY_HOTEL` | `SFH_TAXI` |\r\n119: \r\n120: This says which mission's state reveals a progress-menu entry. It is not evidence that one mission must be\r\n121: completed before the other can start, and the direction of any in-game dependency is not established.\r\n122: \r\n123: ## Unresolved and ambiguous nodes\r\n124: \r\n125: Every edge is unresolved. Specific ambiguities in the nodes themselves:\r\n126: \r\n127: - **Chapter disagreements (5):** `CLB_MEET` (menu chapter 3, texture `CH2